.TH WSH-LAB-MCP 1 "" "wsh-lab" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
wsh-lab-mcp \- MCP server that lets an AI assistant see and drive an allowed VM
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B wsh-lab-mcp \-\-setup
.br
.B wsh-lab-mcp \-\-remove\-venv
.br
.B wsh-lab-mcp
.RB [ \-\-vm
.IR VMID " ...]"
.RB [ \-\-tag
.IR TAG " ...]"
.RB [ \-\-lab
.IR NAME=DIR " ...]"
.RB [ \-\-allow\-reset ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-stop ]
.RB [ \-\-allow\-hmp ]
.RB [ \-\-check ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B wsh-lab-mcp
runs the sparc lab's Model Context Protocol server (mcp_server.py) on stdin and stdout. An MCP
client gets the screen of the VMs named on the command line as an image or as text, can type on
their keyboard or serial console, read the serial output and wait for a pattern. Only the VMs given
with
.BR \-\-vm ,
.B \-\-tag
or
.B \-\-lab
can be touched; resetting, stopping and the QEMU monitor are off unless enabled.
.B \-\-allow\-hmp
gives the client root on the node.
.PP
The server needs the
.B mcp
Python SDK, which Debian does not package.
.B wsh-lab-mcp \-\-setup
(as root, once) creates a venv in
.I /var/lib/wsh-lab/mcp-venv
with the pinned, hash-checked requirements in
.I /usr/share/wsh-lab/mcp-requirements.txt
(pip \-\-require\-hashes, wheels only). It needs the
.B python3-venv
package and network access to PyPI. Run it again after an upgrade of wsh-lab or of python3 when the
wrapper asks for it.
.B apt purge wsh-lab
removes the venv.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR wsh-lab (1),
https://github.com/hwinther/wsh-pve/tree/main/e2e/sparc#mcp-server
